// ISO §12.4.5.6.2 — the ALTERNATE RECORD KEY general format is
//   ALTERNATE RECORD KEY IS data-name-1 [ WITH DUPLICATES ] [ SUPPRESS WHEN literal-1 ]
// The SUPPRESS WHEN phrase (COBOL-2023, Annex E item 42) is parsed at EVERY --std (the superset-grammar
// doctrine, DESIGN-version-conformance-pipeline §2) and introduction-gated post-parse by
// VersionConformancePass.ParseArm.VisitAlternateKeySuppressWhen → Check(AlternateKeySuppressWhen2023).
// `literal` (not nonNumericLiteral) is deliberate: SR7 admits only alphanumeric/national/figurative, but a
// numeric literal must produce a NAMED diagnostic (COBOLNET1578), not a parse error — parse-wide/bind-narrow.
alternateKeyClause
    : ALTERNATE RECORD? KEY? IS? dataReference
      (WITH? DUPLICATES)?
      alternateKeySuppressWhen?
    ;

// ISO §12.4.5.6.2 — [ SUPPRESS WHEN literal-1 ]. SUPPRESS and WHEN are both hard-reserved tokens in all four
// editions (reserved-words.json: SUPPRESS continuous since 1985), so this arm is KEYWORD-LED (DEVLOG 903) and
// cannot be reached by any dataReference/cobolWord path — no DFA hazard.
alternateKeySuppressWhen
    : SUPPRESS WHEN literal
    ;

semanticChanges.change:
At :695-699 replace the capture with:
```
else if (clauses.alternateKeyClause() is { } ak)                       // ISO §12.4.5.6
{
    var (an, aq) = KeyReference(ak.dataReference());
    string? sup = ak.alternateKeySuppressWhen() is { } sw ? BindSuppressLiteral(sw, an, aq, file) : null;
    file.AlternateKeyNames.Add((an, aq, ak.DUPLICATES() is not null, sup));
}
```
Add a private helper `BindSuppressLiteral(AlternateKeySuppressWhenContext sw, string keyName, IReadOnlyList<string> quals, FileModel file)` that returns the decoded suppression string or null on error. Because the alternate key's DataItem is not resolved until the post-build pass (:922), the helper must run the CATEGORY check (SR7) LATE — so capture the raw parse context in the tuple pathway is undesirable; instead: (1) in BindSuppressLiteral decode only the SYNTACTIC parts that need no key type — reject a numericLiteral outright with COBOLNET1578 and reject a multi-character `ALL literal` with COBOLNET1579 (SR7 sentence 2, checked on `figurativeConstant.ALL()` + STRINGLIT/HEXLIT/NATLIT length after decode); (2) return the decoded string WITHOUT width normalization, plus stash the parse context on a parallel `file.AlternateKeySuppressCtx` list (or make the tuple carry `object? SyntaxRef`) so the post-build pass can finish the category check. SIMPLER AND PREFERRED SHAPE (adopt this): make AlternateKeyNames carry `(string Name, IReadOnlyList<string> Qualifiers, bool Duplicates, AlternateKeySuppressWhenContext? SuppressCtx)` and do ALL literal binding in the post-build pass where the DataItem is known — a single site, no split logic.

Add a parse-arm gate as a sibling of VisitSharingClause (:946) / VisitLockModeClause (:951) — place it immediately after VisitLockModeClause:
```
/// <summary>SUPPRESS WHEN literal-1 on the ALTERNATE RECORD KEY clause (ISO §12.4.5.6.2, Annex E item 42) —
/// alternate-key suppression is a COBOL-2023 addition; the bare ALTERNATE RECORD KEY clause (with or without
/// WITH DUPLICATES) is edition-invariant. Recognition-based on the phrase's own rule so a below-2023
/// occurrence names its edition even on a SELECT error path (DEVLOG 724).</summary>
public override object? VisitAlternateKeySuppressWhen(CobolParserCore.AlternateKeySuppressWhenContext ctx)
{ _p.Check(Constructs.AlternateKeySuppressWhen2023, "the SUPPRESS WHEN phrase of the ALTERNATE RECORD KEY clause"); return base.VisitChildren(ctx); }
```
Gate on the DEDICATED rule, not on VisitAlternateKeyClause with a null-check — a dedicated rule guarantees exactly-once firing and is immune to the DEVLOG-736 shared-rule over-fire pattern.

Widen the shim at :300-302 to a 5-argument form:
```
/// <summary>Register one ALTERNATE RECORD KEY window (§12.4.5.6) with its optional key SUPPRESSION value
/// (§12.4.5.6.4 GR6) — <c>CobolFile.AddAlternateKey</c>.</summary>
public static string FileAddAlternateKey(string name, string offset, int width, string dups, string suppress) =>
    $"{nameof(CobolFile)}.{nameof(CobolFile.AddAlternateKey)}({name}, {offset}, {width}, {dups}, {suppress})";
```
Do NOT add an overload — one mechanism per job (feedback_singular_pattern); update the single call site.

Widen the facade at :51-52:
```
/// <summary>Register one ALTERNATE RECORD KEY (§12.4.5.6) in declaration order, with its optional
/// SUPPRESS WHEN key-suppression value (§12.4.5.6.4 GR6; null = no suppression).</summary>
public static void AddAlternateKey(string name, int offset, int length, bool duplicates, string? suppress = null)
    => _reg.AddAlternateKey(name, offset, length, duplicates, suppress);
```

THE behavioral core. Nine concrete edits:
(1) :27 — `private readonly List<(int Off, int Len, bool Dups, string? Suppress)> _alts = [];`
(2) :78 — `public void AddAlternateKey(int offset, int length, bool duplicates, string? suppress = null) => _alts.Add((offset, length, duplicates, suppress));`
(3) NEW helper (place beside KeyOf, ~:450):
```
/// <summary>True when this record's <paramref name="keyIndex"/> alternate key equals that key's SUPPRESS WHEN
/// value (§12.4.5.6.4 GR6): the alternate access path to the record is NOT provided under this key. The
/// comparison is the relation-condition comparison of §14.9.51 GR35 / §8.8.4.2.7 — the shorter operand is
/// treated as if space-extended on the right; the prime key (keyIndex &lt; 0) is never suppressible.</summary>
private bool IsSuppressed(string image, int keyIndex)
{
    if (keyIndex < 0 || _alts[keyIndex].Suppress is not { } lit) return false;
    string k = KeyOf(image, keyIndex);
    int n = Math.Max(k.Length, lit.Length);
    return string.CompareOrdinal(k.PadRight(n, ' '), lit.PadRight(n, ' ')) == 0;
}
```
(4) :441-444 — `Ordered` becomes the ONE visibility filter (§14.9.30 GR21c / §14.9.41 GR17e):
```
private List<KeyedRec> Ordered(int keyIndex) =>
    [.. _recs.Where(r => !IsSuppressed(r.Image, keyIndex))
             .OrderBy(r => KeyOf(r.Image, keyIndex), StringComparer.Ordinal).ThenBy(r => r.Arrival)];
```
This single change makes READ NEXT/PREVIOUS (:172-238), START KEY (:378-414) and StartFirstLast (:418-431) all skip suppressed records, AND makes the GR27 '02' look-ahead (:211-219) inspect only the VISIBLE neighbour — which is correct, because a suppressed record is 'as if it did not exist'.
(5) ReadRandom (:240-266) — add the visibility filter to the format-2 scan (§14.9.30 GR32 + §12.4.5.6.4 GR6 NOTE): change the loop guard to `if (!IsSuppressed(rec.Image, keyIndex) && KeyOf(rec.Image, keyIndex) == value && …)`. If every match is suppressed the loop leaves `found` null and the existing '23' invalid-key path fires — exactly the spec outcome.
(6) Write (:293-301) — per §14.9.51 GR41: skip a suppressed alternate ENTIRELY (no duplicate check, no '02'):
```
for (int i = 0; i < _alts.Count; i++)
{
    if (IsSuppressed(image, i)) continue;   // §14.9.51 GR41 — no access path; suppressed entries never duplicate
    string value = KeyOf(image, i);
    bool exists = _recs.Any(r => !IsSuppressed(r.Image, i) && KeyOf(r.Image, i) == value);
    if (exists && !_alts[i].Dups) return Status = FileStatusCode.DuplicateKey;   // '22' GR40/GR42c
    if (exists) duplicateAlt = true;
}
```
The `!IsSuppressed(r.Image, i)` guard on the EXISTING side is defensive-correct (a suppressed stored value can never equal a non-suppressed incoming value, so it is a no-op) but documents GR41's 'shall not cause a duplicate key condition' explicitly.
(7) Rewrite (:325-338) — same shape, plus the GR24 repositioning:
```
for (int i = 0; i < _alts.Count; i++)
{
    string newValue = KeyOf(image, i);
    bool nowSup = IsSuppressed(image, i), wasSup = IsSuppressed(target.Image, i);
    if (newValue != KeyOf(target.Image, i) || nowSup != wasSup) altChanged = true;   // §14.9.35 GR24b + GR24 suppression clauses
    if (nowSup) continue;                                                            // §14.9.35 GR24 — path withdrawn
    bool exists = _recs.Any(r => !ReferenceEquals(r, target) && !IsSuppressed(r.Image, i) && KeyOf(r.Image, i) == newValue);
    if (exists && !_alts[i].Dups) return Status = FileStatusCode.DuplicateKey;        // '22' GR25c
    if (exists) duplicateAlt = true;
}
```
The `nowSup != wasSup` term implements GR24's 'the record shall be logically (re)positioned' in BOTH directions: leaving suppression re-provides the path and re-positions the record LAST among duplicates (the existing `if (altChanged) target.Arrival = _nextArrival++;` at :341 already realizes 'logically positioned last', GR24b).
(8) DELETE (:347-367) — NO change required and this must be stated in the code comment: DELETE targets the PRIME key and removes the record from `_recs`, which withdraws every access path (suppressed or not) atomically. The spec defines no suppression interaction for DELETE.
(9) Update the class doc-comment (:5-16) to name §12.4.5.6.4 GR6 and state that suppression is realized as a visibility filter on the one `Ordered`/scan derivation, never as a separate index.

grammarChanges.newText:
// PIC Clause — PIC/PICTURE triggers PICMODE in the lexer, which emits a single PIC_STRING token. IS is consumed
// by PICMODE. The EDITING phrase (ISO §13.18.40.2 Format 1, a COBOL-2023 introduction) is the clause's own
// repeatable optional tail — it belongs to the PICTURE clause, not to dataDescriptionClause, because character-1
// is meaningless without character-string-1 and the binder needs them as one unit. No PICMODE change is required:
// a PIC string can contain no space and PIC_STRING pops the mode, so `PIC L999.99 EDITING "L"` already yields
// PIC_STRING="L999.99" then DEFAULT-mode EDITING. UNGATED superset parse; the 2023 introduction gate is
// VersionConformancePass ParseArm.VisitEditingClause -> COBOLNET0900 (the dynamicLengthClause pattern). Every
// syntax rule (SR8-SR12, SR24-SR26) is bind-checked in DataBinder/PictureAnalyzer.
pictureClause
    : PIC PIC_STRING editingClause*
    ;

// EDITING character-1 [ IS literal-1 | FOR { NEGATIVE IS literal-2 | POSITIVE IS literal-3 } ] ... (§13.18.40.2)
// character-1 is written as a one-character alphanumeric (or national, SR9) literal — `EDITING "L"`. The
// `IS`/`FOR` group is optional per the diagram; the bare form has no defined general rule and is REJECTED at
// bind (COBOLNET1586), never silently accepted. The FOR tail is `(...)+` rather than the diagram's exclusive
// stack because SR12a ("Literal-2 and literal-3 shall occupy the same number of character positions") and
// SR12c ("If only POSITIVE is specified... If only NEGATIVE is specified...") only have meaning if one phrase
// may carry both — the superset parse admits both orders and repetition; duplicates reject at bind
// (COBOLNET1587).
editingClause
    : EDITING literal (IS? literal | FOR editingSignPhrase+)?
    ;

editingSignPhrase
    : NEGATIVE IS? literal
    | POSITIVE IS? literal
    ;

semanticChanges.change:
THE CARRIER DECISION. The per-item editing map is carried as a CANONICAL ENCODED STRING, exactly parallel to the existing `EditMask` string — NOT as an array/collection. Rationale: (a) PicInfo is a `sealed record` whose synthesized value-equality would degrade to reference equality on an array member; (b) every emitter site already knows how to emit a C# string literal via `EmitText.CsLiteral`; (c) the two COMPILE-TIME passthroughs (RuntimeApi.MaskScale, RuntimeApi.EditCompose) keep working unchanged; (d) it composes with the existing named-argument `EditCfgArgs` suffix convention. Define:

  public readonly record struct EditingSymbol(char Symbol, string PosText, string NegText, bool SignSensitive);

  public static class EditingSymbols
  {
      // Canonical spec encoding: symbols joined by ''; each symbol is
      //   <char-1> '' <posText> '' <negText> '' ('S' | 'F')
      // 'F' = the IS form (simple insertion, unconditional: PosText == NegText == literal-1, never floats).
      // 'S' = the FOR form (extended editing sign control, sign-selected, may float).
      // Symbols appear in PICTURE-position order (leftmost character-1 first) — the SR25 ordering.
      public static string Encode(IReadOnlyList<EditingSymbol> symbols);
      public static EditingSymbol[] Decode(string? spec);          // null/empty -> Array.Empty
      public static EditingSymbol[] Get(string? spec);             // Decode + a small static ConcurrentDictionary cache keyed on spec (parsed once per distinct spec per process)
      public static int IndexOf(EditingSymbol[] syms, char c);     // -1 when not an es symbol
  }

Width invariant enforced at Encode: PosText.Length == NegText.Length for every symbol (the binder guarantees it via SR12a and the SR12c spaces default). Encode/Decode are the ONE definition — the compiler builds the spec with Encode and the runtime reads it with Get.

Add, immediately after the `EditMask` property (line 207):

  /// <summary>The PICTURE clause's EDITING phrases (ISO §13.18.40.2 Format 1, a COBOL-2023 introduction),
  /// canonically encoded by <c>EditingSymbols.Encode</c> — the per-ITEM companion to <see cref="EditMask"/>
  /// (the program-level currency/comma config rides EmitContext.EditCfgArgs instead). Null when the entry has
  /// no EDITING phrase, which is every pre-2023 program — so the encoded-string shape keeps PicInfo's record
  /// value-equality intact and every emit site keeps emitting one C# string literal.</summary>
  public string? EditingSpec { get; init; }

No change to the positional parameter list (adding a positional member would churn every construction site). `Length` continues to be the EXPANDED character-position count — see the PictureAnalyzer change.

Add an `IReadOnlyList<EditingSymbol>? editing = null` parameter to `Analyze` (after `explicitUsage`). Changes, in order of the method's existing flow:
  (a) SR2 WHITELIST (lines 61-88). Before the `default:` arm add `case var c when editing is not null && editing.Any(e => e.Symbol == c): break;` — an es character is an allowable picture symbol. Any character-1 NOT in the whitelist and not in the map still falls to COBOLNET0808 unchanged.
  (b) SR8 CHARACTER-1 DOMAIN (new, before the whitelist loop). For each mapped symbol: reject COBOLNET1578 unless it is a basic letter AND not in {A,B,C,D,E,N,P,R,S,V,X,Z} AND != cs (the resolved CURRENCY SIGN picture symbol, not just '$'). Legal set: F G H I J K L M O Q T U W Y (and lowercase, folded by ExpandRepeats' ToUpperInvariant).
  (c) SR10 (new): each mapped symbol must occur >= 1 time in `expanded` -> COBOLNET1579.
  (d) SR12b + the SR12 trailing bullet (new): if ANY mapped symbol is SignSensitive, `expanded` may contain ONLY mapped symbols and {9, '.', cs, P, V, Z} -> COBOLNET1583. This subsumes 'no editing sign control symbols' (+ - CR DB) and 'not for a floating-point edited item' (E).
  (e) SR24/SR25 (new): count SignSensitive symbols; > 2 -> COBOLNET1584. When exactly 2, the first EDITING phrase's symbol must be the LEFTMOST es occurrence in `expanded` and the second the RIGHTMOST -> COBOLNET1584 (same code, distinct message).
  (f) CATEGORY. Extend `anyEdit` (line 225) with `|| (editing?.Count > 0)` so an es symbol makes the item numeric-edited (GR13a). Extend the `anyAlpha` branch's `edited` flag (line 232) with es presence so `PIC XXXLXXX EDITING "L" IS "--"` is ALPHANUMERIC-EDITED (GR7) and gets an EditMask. Same for the national-edited leg at line 129 (GR10) — but see (i).
  (g) SR12 CATEGORY RESTRICTION (new): if any mapped symbol is SignSensitive and the resolved category is not NumericEdited (nor Numeric) -> COBOLNET1585.
  (h) SIZE — the GR14 'es' rule, THE most error-prone change. Replace the numeric-edited `Length:` expression at line 249 and the alphanumeric one at line 234 with a shared helper:

      static int EditedWidth(string expanded, IReadOnlyList<EditingSymbol>? es, char cs)
      {
          int n = 0;
          foreach (char c in expanded) { if (c is 'V' or 'S' or 'P') continue; if (es-symbol(c)) continue; n++; }
          foreach (var s in es ?? []) {
              int occ = expanded.Count(c => c == s.Symbol);
              bool floats = s.SignSensitive && occ >= 2;
              n += floats ? s.PosText.Length + (occ - 1)   // GR14: one occurrence of the literal + one char per repetition
                          : occ * s.PosText.Length;         // GR14: fixed/simple — each occurrence counts its literal
          }
          return n;
      }

     VERIFIED against Annex D.24: `PIC LLLL9,99 EDITING "L" FOR NEGATIVE IS "DEBIT "` -> 6 + 3 (floating es) + 4 ('9', ',', '9', '9') = 13, exactly the spec's stated 13 ("6 for the first 'L', 3 for the next three, and 4 for the numbers"). And `PIC L999.99 EDITING "L" FOR NEGATIVE IS "DEBIT "` -> 6 + 6 = 12, matching D.24's 12-byte results.
  (i) SR9 NATIONAL LEG: if usage is national or `expanded` contains 'N', the literals shall be national literals. National-edited is ALREADY staged not-implemented at line 133 (`StagedNotImplemented(edition, Constructs.NationalEdited2002, …)`), so national EDITING inherits that COBOLNET0899 for free. Add an explicit COBOLNET1581 when a NON-national literal class is supplied for a national subject (SR9 is a syntax rule, checkable independently of the staging).
  (j) Emit `EditingSpec = EditingSymbols.Encode(editing)` on the returned PicInfo for the NumericEdited, Alphanumeric-edited and National-edited branches.

TWO edits. (1) In `ParseArm`, beside VisitDynamicLengthClause (line 714-718), add:

  /// <summary>The EDITING phrase of the PICTURE clause (ISO §13.18.40.2 Format 1; Annex E.3.3 item 19) — a
  /// COBOL-2023 introduction. PARSE-arm (recognition) like the ANY LENGTH / DYNAMIC LENGTH gates: every SR8-SR12
  /// / SR24-SR26 violation drops or recovers the map in the binder, so a bound-arm home would lose the 0900 on
  /// exactly the declaration-error paths (the DEVLOG-724 lesson).</summary>
  public override object? VisitEditingClause(CobolParserCore.EditingClauseContext ctx)
  {
      _p.Check(Constructs.EditingPhrase2023, "the EDITING phrase of the PICTURE clause");
      return base.VisitChildren(ctx);
  }

  DO NOT wrap in `InGatedDataEntry(ctx)`: that guard exists because BASED/ANY LENGTH/DYNAMIC LENGTH can be
  mis-attached to a level-66/88 entry where a 0900 would be spurious. editingClause is a child of pictureClause,
  which never appears on a 66/88 entry, and it DOES legitimately appear in report and screen entries — where the
  gate is equally correct. An InGatedDataEntry guard would silently drop the gate for RW/screen pictures.

(2) Add `CobolLexer.EDITING` to `CheckedTokenTypes` (line 1513-1543) with the comment: "§13.18.40.2 (2023): the
  sole keyword slot is the editingClause head, a direct token immediately after PIC_STRING, never a name slot —
  position-blind safe (the CONSTANT / AS / PROGRAM-POINTER precedent)." This makes the existing §8.9 funnel emit
  COBOLNET0901 at --std 2023 for `01 EDITING PIC 9.` while leaving it a legal user word at 85/2002/2014.

EditCfgArgs (line 48-51) is PROGRAM-level and MUST NOT carry the map. Add a sibling per-item helper on EmitContext:

  /// <summary>The per-ITEM EDITING-phrase argument for a generated CobolEdit call (ISO §13.18.40.2) — empty for
  /// every item without an EDITING phrase, so ordinary generated code is byte-identical. Composes AFTER
  /// EditCfgArgs (named argument, order-independent).</summary>
  public string EditArgs(PicInfo pic) => pic.EditingSpec is null ? "" : $", editing: {EmitText.CsLiteral(pic.EditingSpec)}";

Emit the spec as an inline string literal, not a hoisted field: EditingSymbols.Get caches the parse per distinct
spec string, so the per-call cost is one dictionary probe on an interned literal — no allocation, no generated
static state, and the emitted code stays readable.

grammarChanges.newText:
// Format 3 (exception-checking), ISO §14.9.28.2: PERFORM [WITH LOCATION] imperative-statement-1
//   { WHEN {EXCEPTION [file-name-1… | INPUT | OUTPUT | I-O | EXTEND] | exception-name-1… |
//           exception-name-2 FILE file-name-2…} imperative-statement-2 }…
//   [WHEN OTHER EXCEPTION imperative-statement-3] [WHEN COMMON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-4]
//   [FINALLY imperative-statement-5] END-PERFORM.
// ONE arm rule with OTHER/COMMON FIRST so a cobolWord-shaped exception-name can never swallow them
// (the DEVLOG-736 shared-rule over-fire guard); arm ORDER/uniqueness is a binder rule, not a grammar rule.
| PERFORM (WITH? LOCATION)? statementBlock+ performWhenArm+ performFinally? END_PERFORM

performWhenSelector
    // The F1-shaped selector (§14.9.28.2: EXCEPTION [ {file-name-1}… | INPUT | OUTPUT | IO | EXTEND ]).
    // The format figure writes "IO"; the actual COBOL word is I-O (the existing I_O token via useOnTarget) —
    // do NOT introduce a bare IO token. The target is OPTIONAL per the figure's bracket.
    : EXCEPTION useOnTarget?
    // The F3-shaped selector: exception-name-1… | exception-name-2 FILE file-name-2…
    | useEcEntry+
    ;

performFinally
    : FINALLY statementBlock+
    ;

semanticChanges.change:
The chaining funnel and the generated selector.
  (A) Change `EcDispatchExpr(ecNameExpr, fileExpr)` (:36-37) to consult DispatchState: when `PerformWhenScopeId is int n`, return `$"__PfDisp{n}({ecNameExpr}, {fileExpr})"`; otherwise the existing `__EcDispatch(...)`/`-3`. This is the SINGLE funnel every raise site already uses (the fatal-gate catch at :132, EmitRaise at :164, EmitSizeHandling, __IoCheckEc at :349), so ALL raise sites become WHEN-aware with one edit — no per-verb changes.
  (B) Every raise-site epilogue that today reads `if (__r{id} >= 0) { __pc = __r{id}; break; }` gains ONE line ABOVE it, emitted only when a Format-3 scope is active: `if (__r{id} == -4) goto __pfEnd{n};   // EXIT PERFORM in a WHEN handler (§14.9.14.4 GR4)`. Add a small helper `EmitDispatchEpilogue(int id, bool fatal)` and route the four existing sites through it (the refactor-first rule).
  (C) New `public void EmitPerformWhenSelector(BoundExceptionCheckingPerform p, CodeWriter w)` — generates, as a private method on the program class beside __EcDispatch:
```
private int __PfWhen{N}(string __ec, string __f)
{
    // §14.9.28.4 GR17 -> §14.9.49.4 GR3a-3g tier order; source order breaks ties WITHIN a tier
    // GR3a — WHEN EXCEPTION file-name-1 arms
    if (__f == "<fileKey>" && __ec.StartsWith("EC-I-O", StringComparison.Ordinal)) { <arm body>; goto __common; }
    // GR3b — WHEN EXCEPTION INPUT|OUTPUT|I-O|EXTEND arms (runtime open-mode test, same source __IoCheck uses)
    if (__ec.StartsWith("EC-I-O", StringComparison.Ordinal) && FileRegistry.ModeOf(__f) == <ordinal>) { <arm body>; goto __common; }
    // GR3c — file-scoped level-3 exception-name arms
    if (__f == "<fileKey>" && __ec == "<EC-NAME>") { <arm body>; goto __common; }
    // GR3d — file-scoped level-2 arms
    if (__f == "<fileKey>" && ExceptionCatalog.UnderLevel2(__ec, "<EC-2>")) { <arm body>; goto __common; }
    // GR3e — level-3 arms;  GR3f — level-2 arms;  GR3g — the EC-ALL arm
    ...
    // GR18 — WHEN OTHER EXCEPTION
    { <other body>; goto __common; }        // emitted only when WHEN OTHER is present
    return -3;                              // no arm matched -> the USE tier (§14.6.13.1.3 #5 / §14.6.13.1.4 #3)
__common:
    <common body>                           // GR19, emitted only when WHEN COMMON is present
    return -1;                              // handled; GR20 nonfatal -> caller continues after the raising statement
}
```
Copy the tier machinery from `EmitDispatchSelector` (:252-287) verbatim in structure — `L3`/`L2` predicates, `ExceptionCatalog.UnderLevel2`, `FileKeyExpr`. The BARE `WHEN EXCEPTION` (no target) emits in the GR3f band as `if (ExceptionCatalog.UnderLevel2(__ec, "EC-I-O"))`. Handler bodies emit with `DispatchState.InPerformWhenHandler = true` and `PerformWhenScopeId = null` (GR21 — a handler is NOT inside the checked region; combined with the bind-time SuppressAll frame no handler statement carries a wrapper anyway, so this is belt-and-braces).
  (D) New `public void EmitPerformWhenChain(BoundExceptionCheckingPerform p, CodeWriter w)` generating
```
private int __PfDisp{N}(string __ec, string __f)
{
    int __r = __PfWhen{N}(__ec, __f);
    return __r == -3 ? {enclosingChainExpr} : __r;   // GR17: a matched WHEN IGNORES the USE declarative
}
```
where `enclosingChainExpr` is `__PfDisp{M}(__ec, __f)` for the next enclosing Format-3 scope (§14.6.13.1.4 #2 — an OUTER PERFORM also 'contains' the statement, so its arms get their chance) or `__EcDispatch(__ec, __f)` / the literal `-3` at the outermost level.

Add `public void EmitExceptionCheckingPerform(BoundExceptionCheckingPerform p)` emitting IN-LINE inside the current dispatcher switch case (the handlers live in generated METHODS; only the body and FINALLY are inline):
```
// PERFORM (exception-checking) — ISO §14.9.28 Format 3
{ var save = (dispatch.PerformWhenScopeId, dispatch.PerformWhenEndLabel);
  dispatch.PerformWhenScopeId = p.Id; dispatch.PerformWhenEndLabel = $"__pfEnd{p.Id}";
  foreach (var s in p.Body) statements.EmitStatement(s);
  (dispatch.PerformWhenScopeId, dispatch.PerformWhenEndLabel) = save; }
w.Line($"__pfEnd{p.Id}: ;");                       // GR16/GR20 — the end of the PERFORM begins here
foreach (var s in p.Finally) statements.EmitStatement(s);   // GR16 + NOTE 8
```
CRITICAL: FINALLY is emitted as PLAIN SEQUENTIAL STATEMENTS, never a C# `try/finally`. §14.6.13.1.3 #4 terminates the run unit abnormally after the WHEN (+COMMON) handler on the FATAL path, so a fatal exception must propagate PAST the FINALLY code — which a plain sequential emission gives for free (the `throw;` at the raise site skips it), and which a C# `finally` would wrongly run. An EXIT PERFORM inside imperative-statement-5 is bound to BoundExitPerformWhen with the same scope id and, since PerformWhenEndLabel is restored to null/outer by then, must emit a jump PAST the FINALLY block — allocate a SECOND label `__pfAfter{N}:` immediately after the FINALLY block and set `dispatch.PerformWhenEndLabel = $"__pfAfter{p.Id}"` while emitting the FINALLY body (§14.9.28.4 GR16: "Any EXIT PERFORM statement within imperative-statement-5 transfers control to an implicit CONTINUE statement following the END-PERFORM").
Also register the two generated methods (__PfWhen{N}, __PfDisp{N}) with the program-class member emitter so they land beside __EcDispatch.

