// The MCS asynchronous-messaging statements (ISO §14.9.31 RECEIVE, §14.9.38 SEND Formats 1–2; and the
// X3.23-1985 Communication Module forms) — a PROCESSOR-DEPENDENT facility (A.3 item 4) COBOL.NET does not
// support. §4.2.6 requires only that the element be NAMED at compile time and explicitly excuses diagnosing
// syntax errors WITHIN the unsupported syntax, so the operand tail is swallowed rather than parsed: this ONE
// rule covers RECEIVE, both SEND formats, END-RECEIVE/END-SEND, the CONTINUE AFTER / RAISING / ON EXCEPTION
// phrases, and the '85 CD-name forms, at zero grammar cost. `sentence : statement+ DOT` (:571) guarantees the
// only DOT in a procedure body is the sentence terminator, so the swallow can never cross a sentence boundary
// (the `authorContent : ~DOT+` precedent at :277).
mcsFacilityStatement
    : (RECEIVE | SEND) (~DOT)*
    ;

// The VALIDATE statement (ISO §14.9.50) — an OPTIONAL (§4.2.7 / A.4.14) and, at 2023, OBSOLETE (§4.2.13 /
// F.2 item 5) facility COBOL.NET does not support; F.2's disposition paragraph exempts obsolete-AND-optional
// elements from the support obligation. The operand list is SWALLOWED, not parsed as dataReference+, on
// purpose: binding real operand trees would produce spurious name-resolution errors inside syntax we are
// explicitly excused from diagnosing (§4.2.6/§4.2.13) and would make the §14.9.50.3 SR1-SR6 rules look
// half-enforced. Nothing downstream consumes the operands (the statement binds to BoundNop).
validateFacilityStatement
    : VALIDATE (~DOT)*
    ;

