grammarChanges.newText:
// ISO §12.4.5.7 COLLATING SEQUENCE clause of the file control entry — the ordering (and, per §12.4.5.12 GR1,
// the EQUALITY) of an indexed file's record keys. §12.4.5.1 lists it as `[ collating-sequence-clause ] …`
// in the INDEXED format ONLY, and it is REPEATABLE (one file-level Format 1 + any number of key-level
// Format 2 clauses; SR3 caps the file-level ones at one, enforced at bind).
// Format 1 (file-level): COLLATING SEQUENCE { IS alphabet-name-1 [ alphabet-name-2 ]
//                                           | { FOR ALPHANUMERIC IS alphabet-name-1
//                                             | FOR NATIONAL     IS alphabet-name-2 } … }
// Format 2 (key-level):  COLLATING SEQUENCE OF { data-name-1 | record-key-name-1 } … IS alphabet-name-3
// The Format-2 `FOR ALPHANUMERIC/NATIONAL IS` spelling is not in Figure 2 but IS written by the standard's
// own Annex D.13.3 example — accepted as a permissive superset (the class is decided by the KEY's class per
// SR7, so the FOR word carries no extra meaning here and is captured only for the SR7 cross-check).
// KEYWORD-LED on the reserved COLLATING token — never an IDENTIFIER-led arm (DEVLOG 903). Unlike
// sortCollatingPhrase, COLLATING is REQUIRED here: the L5 CCVS leniency exists only because the 85-era CCVS
// suite omits it in SORT/MERGE, and this clause does not exist in COBOL-85, so there is no such corpus.
fileCollatingSequenceClause
    : COLLATING SEQUENCE
      ( OF dataReferenceList (collatingForPhrase | IS? cobolWord)   // Format 2 (key-level)
      | collatingForPhrase+                                          // Format 1, FOR forms
      | IS? cobolWord cobolWord?                                     // Format 1, IS alphabet-name-1 [alphabet-name-2]
      )
    ;

